1
00:00:01,603 --> 00:00:04,723
Soroban has a state model the EVM never had.

2
00:00:04,723 --> 00:00:07,844
Entries expire, get archived, and can be brought back.

3
00:00:08,677 --> 00:00:14,572
Foundry and Echidna cannot model any of that, so
the most dangerous bugs ship straight to mainnet.

4
00:00:15,695 --> 00:00:17,082
Z-FUZZ hunts them first.

5
00:00:17,082 --> 00:00:22,283
I connect my own Stellar wallet, with no
password and no seed phrase handed over.

6
00:00:23,088 --> 00:00:27,943
My scan credit here arrived as real
USDC through a SEP-6 Turkish Lira anchor.

7
00:00:29,594 --> 00:00:30,904
I sign the SEP-10 challenge myself.

8
00:00:34,047 --> 00:00:36,803
The anchor now trusts this wallet, not our server.

9
00:00:37,715 --> 00:00:38,755
Now the scan.

10
00:00:38,755 --> 00:00:43,956
I pick two lanes: state lifecycle, and composability
against a live Blend v2 lending pool.

11
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:46,036
Then I let the agent go.

12
00:00:48,728 --> 00:00:53,582
Free local tools triage first, so the
paid agent only works where it matters.

13
00:00:53,582 --> 00:00:57,743
It writes a Rust test and
runs it in a sealed container.

14
00:00:57,743 --> 00:00:58,567
And there it is.

15
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:00,580
The test failed.

16
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:06,475
It read an archived entry after its TTL expired
and borrowed far too much against the pool.

17
00:01:06,475 --> 00:01:08,209
Left one hundred, right zero.

18
00:01:08,209 --> 00:01:10,301
That is a proof, not a warning.

19
00:01:11,158 --> 00:01:13,238
Every finding ships with the fix.

20
00:01:13,238 --> 00:01:17,614
The patch checks entry liveness and restores the archived
state before reading collateral, closing the resurrection path.

21
00:01:18,506 --> 00:01:24,401
Run it again, and a passing contract earns an
on chain Z-FUZZ Audited stamp anyone can verify.

22
00:01:24,401 --> 00:01:26,481
Break it here, not on mainnet.
